      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s early economy boasted a diverse array of mills—woolen, silk, shoe pegs, mirror frames, and even powder kegs—powered by Great Brook.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nhmagazine.com/the-villages-of-antrim/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Tuttle Library, built in 1908 in Colonial Revival style, now houses the Antrim Historical Society’s museum and archives.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nhmagazine.com/the-villages-of-antrim/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Antrim’s main village, South Antrim, grew around commercial activity and remains the town’s focal point today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antrim%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every July 4th, residents gather at Memorial Park’s bandstand to read the Declaration of Independence, a tradition started by the local historical society.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nhmagazine.com/the-villages-of-antrim/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s highest point, Bald Mountain at 2,037 feet, overlooks Willard Pond and offers panoramic views of the Contoocook Valley.",
